  {
    "text": "Yes. Clarithromycin is a strong inhibitor of CYP3A4, and simvastatin is extensively metabolised by CYP3A4. Co-administration therefore reduces simvastatin's metabolism, increasing its plasma exposure. Because simvastatin's myopathy risk is dose-dependent (concentration-related), elevated levels raise the risk of myopathy and rhabdomyolysis. This is a well-recognised, clinically significant pharmacokinetic interaction; concurrent use is generally avoided or requires an alternative statin/antibiotic.\n\nMECHANISM: CYP3A4 — clarithromycin inhibits CYP3A4, decreasing simvastatin metabolism and increasing its exposure\nFINAL ANSWER: YES",
